      "id": "",
      "title": "MISR Views the Moon",
      "url": "",
      "publishedDate": "2003-04-16T00:00:00.000Z",
      "author": "Jet Propulsion Laboratory ",
      "summary": "On April 16, 2003, NASA's Terra spacecraft performed a special calibration maneuver involving a backward somersault to enable MISR's instruments to capture images of the Moon's surface. During this 16-minute event, all nine MISR cameras obtained images of the waxing gibbous Moon, providing detailed views of lunar features such as Mare Crisium, Mare Tranquillitatis, Copernicus crater, Mare Imbrium, and Tycho crater. These images, taken without radiometric adjustments, help calibrate the spacecraft's instruments and enhance lunar observations.",
