      "id": "",
      "title": "APOD: 2003 April 16 - Magna Bubbles from Mt Etna",
      "url": "",
      "publishedDate": "2003-04-16T00:00:00.000Z",
      "summary": "The webpage features an astronomical image from April 16, 2003, showcasing magma bubbles from Mount Etna's 2001 eruption. The photograph captures hot magma bubbles, some over one meter across, expelled during the eruption. Mount Etna, a highly active basalt volcano in Sicily, Italy, is notable for its size and activity, and is studied for its similarities to Martian volcanoes due to its basaltic composition. The image highlights volcanic processes relevant to planetary geology and provides insight into volcanic activity on Earth and potential parallels on Mars.",
